3. Top K Frequent Elements

Code:
class Solution {
    public int[] topKFrequent(int[] nums, int k) {

        // 1. Count frequency
        HashMap<Integer, Integer> map = new HashMap<>();

        for (int num : nums) {
            map.put(num, map.getOrDefault(num, 0) + 1);
        }

        // 2. Create buckets
        List<Integer>[] buckets = new ArrayList[nums.length + 1];

        for (int num : map.keySet()) {

            int frequency = map.get(num);

            if (buckets[frequency] == null) {
                buckets[frequency] = new ArrayList<>();
            }

            buckets[frequency].add(num);
        }

        // 3. Get top k frequent elements
        int[] result = new int[k];
        int index = 0;

        for (int frequency = buckets.length - 1;
             frequency >= 0 && index < k;
             frequency--) {

            if (buckets[frequency] != null) {
                for (int num : buckets[frequency]) {
                    result[index] = num;
                    index++;

                    if (index == k) {
                        break;
                    }
                }
            }
        }

        return result;
    }
}

Approach:
Use a HashMap to count the frequency of each number. Then create frequency buckets where the index represents the frequency of an element. Place each number into its corresponding bucket. Finally, traverse the buckets from the highest frequency to the lowest and add elements to the result until k elements are collected.

Time Complexity: O(n)
Space Complexity: O(n)

Optimal Code 1:

class Solution {
    public int[] topKFrequent(int[] nums, int k) {
        Map<Integer, Integer> map = new HashMap<>();
        for(int num : nums){
            map.put(num, map.getOrDefault(num, 0)+1);
        }
        PriorityQueue<Integer> queue = new PriorityQueue<>((a,b)->map.get(a)-map.get(b));
        for(int key : map.keySet()){
            queue.offer(key);
            if(queue.size()>k) queue.poll();
        }
        int[] ans = new int[k];
        for(int i=k-1;i>=0;i--){
            ans[i] = queue.poll();
        }
        return ans;
   }
}

Approach:
Use a HashMap to count the frequency of each number. Then use a Min-Heap (PriorityQueue) to keep track of the k most frequent elements. Add each number to the heap, and if the heap size exceeds k, remove the least frequent element. Finally, extract the remaining k elements into the result array.

Time Complexity: O(n log k)
Space Complexity: O(n + k)

Where:

n = number of elements in nums
k = number of most frequent elements required.

